<rtept lat="51.3485300000" lon="-1.7748000000">
<time>2025-07-30T08:36:05Z</time>
<name>Start to Bridge 112</name>
<desc><![CDATA[Begin standing in the Pewsey Wharf car park alongside the Kennet and Avon Canal. Now just a cafe-bistro and car park, this was once the wharf serving the village of Pewsey. It was the village's main trade link with the outside world with its two-storey brick warehouse to store the goods. 

Standing facing the canal, turn right (passing between the canal and the old warehouse bistro). Keep ahead through the gate to join the canal towpath with the canal running on the left. This is a popular mooring spot, so you are likely to be passing a range of canal boats and barges. Follow the towpath for some distance.

The Kennet and Avon Canal was built between 1794 and 1810 to link the River Kennet (which itself links to the River Thames) with the River Avon at Bath. It created an important east-west trade link. It was used to carry iron, coal, stone and timber from Bristol and to bring luxuries like tobacco and spirits from London. 

Pass under the first bridge (Bridge 113) and continue on the towpath. To the right now is Jones's Mill Nature Reserve, a wonderful secluded wetland. The peaty soil is fed by natural springs to support wet woodland, ponds and wet grassland. In the past a watermill (Johun's Myll of 1552) was believed to have been sited here (hence the name) but in more recent times the site has been used to graze dairy cattle and for growing watercress. Today, the reserve is grazed by Belted Galloway cattle to maintain the balance of the habitat which supports water voles, water shrews, herons, kingfishers, dragonflies and marsh orchids. You will have a chance to explore the site later should you wish.

Continue only as far as the second bridge, Bridge 112 (a total distance of just over a mile so far).]]></desc>
<sym>Flag</sym>
